The height of the first cylinder is $\blueD{\dfrac{V}{\pi r^2}}$.

Let's take $\dfrac{V}{\pi \goldD r^2}$ and replace $\goldD r$ with $\goldD{2r}$:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{V}{\pi (\goldD{2r})^2}&=\dfrac{V}{\pi 2^2r^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{V}{4\pi r^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac14\left(\blueD{\dfrac{V}{\pi r^2}}\right)
\end{align}$

We started with an expression where $r$ is increased by a factor of $2$ and ended with the overall expression decreased by a factor of $4$.
